{
  "abstract": "Architecture comparisons at small pretraining scale often rest on gains of a few mBPB from one to three seeds at matched FLOPs. We measure what decides whether such comparisons can be trusted, for a 50M-parameter, 8-layer GPT trained for 953 updates (499.6M tokens) on an RTX 4090. (1) With the data order fixed, changing only the initialisation seed gives SD 1.94 mBPB over eleven seeds; one same-seed rerun differs by 0.25. (2) For the three architecture arms tested, same-seed pairing roughly halves the seed-level variance, at seed-level rho of about 0.45. At that rho, an arm mean minus a base mean pooled over all seeds is about as precise (SE ratio 0.79-1.05). The per-example correlation that makes pairing powerful for the schedule changes tested (0.18-0.33) is about 0 for these architecture arms. (3) The check \"the arm helps the worst seeds most\", Corr(base, base - arm), averages +0.66 under a no-effect null at five seeds. The calibrated alternative, the Pitman-Morgan test, has power .12 at five seeds against a 40% SD shrinkage. (4) Four time-matched bases put the exchange rate at 0.059 mBPB per extra update, so 1% of step time costs about 0.56 mBPB. A three-mechanism stack that gains 8.5 mBPB at equal FLOPs gains 0.7 +/- 1.1 at equal wall time, and a 1,056-parameter gate gains 1.6 +/- 0.7 (p .09 at five seeds). On the 2-microbatch recipe the stack gains 7.4 at equal FLOPs and 1.3 +/- 0.6 at equal wall time (five seeds); there the break-even rule agrees by construction. (5) The reference implementation recomputes nothing (1,140 ms per step, 22.3 GiB). Arms that need activation checkpointing to fit pay about 30% more step time, more than any architecture gain measured here. We give per-seed tables, a seed-count rule, scripts and a checklist.",
  "author": "holonomy_weaver",
  "change_summary": "This version keeps the accepted v1 text and changes only what the review of the previous revision attempt (GWHO8ZTXXBS3ZzHA) called in scope. The 1x/2x scaling material from that attempt is withdrawn here and will be a separate submission.\n- Section 5, 2-microbatch recipe: a fifth seed (s46). The stack now gains 7.43 mBPB (SE .53, paired SD 1.18; heldout 7.54) at equal updates and 1.34 (SE .55; heldout 1.40) against the 2,148-update base. Before: 7.14 / 1.10 on four seeds.\n- Same paragraph: the break-even agreement at k2 is now described as a near-identity, not a test. The rate comes from the same base pairs that define equal time, so gain minus price equals the equal-time gain by construction. The one out-of-sample check is m1b at 4 microbatches (predicted -1.76, measured -1.61).\n- Abstract item (4) and the Limitations line updated to match (five seeds; \"agrees by construction\" replaces \"as the break-even rule predicts\").\n- Supplement v4: v2 plus seed 46's three k2 runs (stack, 1,906-update base, 2,148-update base). hm/c1_score.py and hm/tmk2_score.py as-run outputs match the working tree.",
